 🐪 Good Wednesday afternoon. Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 598 words, a 2½-min. read. Thanks to Sheryl Miller for copy editing . 

 ⚡️   Scoop:   President Trump will attend a "New Golden Age Summit" in Washington tomorrow to hail the U.S. as a leader in "super intelligence," science and space. The administration says the summit "will bring together leading scientists and innovators to examine these changes and catalyze whole-of-nation efforts," Axios' Maria Curi and Alex Isenstadt report . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing — Report: ChatGPT for Teens poses &quot;unacceptable risk&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios Common Sense Media says OpenAI's new ChatGPT for Teens poses an "unacceptable risk," Axios' Ina Fried reports. 

 That's after the nonprofit, which rates and recommends family media, found shortcomings in safeguards meant to protect younger users. 🧑‍💻 The group's Youth AI Safety Institute tested more than 4,000 prompts on accounts registered to 13- to 17-year-olds.

 It found that ChatGPT generally did not provide instructions facilitating suicide or self-harm, eating disorders, or sexual or romantic role-play — but says it was less reliable at recognizing when a teen needed outside help. ChatGPT missed more than 1 in 4 instances in which its testers determined a crisis referral was warranted, the group says. The group also says that on more than a dozen newly created, parent-linked accounts, testers could spend up to an hour discussing suicide, self-harm or disordered eating without triggering a parental alert.

 And it found that "study mode" — meant to help students work through problems rather than deliver immediate answers — could be circumvented. 🤔 The other side: OpenAI says it has issues with Common Sense Media's methodology and findings.

 A spokesperson tells Axios: "We welcome rigorous independent evaluation, but we do not believe Common Sense Media's testing accurately reflects how ChatGPT's teen safeguards work in practice." Go deeper . 

 
 
